{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794535867, "title": "dev/photo-rename \u00b7 not-ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Write a Python script that renames my photos by the date they were taken\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[9.927, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/rename_photos.py)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[14.053, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the script to `<workspace>/rename_photos.py`. I haven't run it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **How it works**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - It reads the EXIF \"date taken\" from each photo. If a photo has none, it uses the file's modification date\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and flags that in the output.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Files are renamed to `YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS.ext`, for example `2024-07-14_15-32-08.jpg`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - If two photos share a timestamp, the second gets `_1`, the next `_2`, and so on, so nothing is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  overwritten.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - It skips files that already have a date name, so running it again is safe.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - It handles jpg, jpeg, png, tif, tiff, heic and webp files.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Usage**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```bash\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pip install Pillow            # plus pillow-heif if you have HEIC files\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip            # preview only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip --apply    # do the rename\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip -r --apply # include subfolders\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  By default it only prints what it would rename, so run it that way first and check the list.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Tell me if you'd prefer a different name format, such as a folder per month or the original name kept as a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  suffix.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[18.273, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[33.711, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I'll extend it with the features that matter most for real photo libraries: video support, companion files,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  better date fallbacks, duplicate detection, folder organizing and an undo log. This time I'll also test it.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[54.834, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/rename_photos.py)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[55.123, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file, ran 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(python3 -c \"import PIL; print(PIL.__version__)\" 2>&1; which ffprobe)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[55.688, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file, ran 1 shell command\u001b[0m\u001b[31m · 1 failed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(python3 -c \"import PIL; print(PIL.__version__)\" 2>&1; which ffprobe)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[57.654, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Pillow isn't installed, so I'll test in a throwaway virtualenv.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
